   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-precalculus",
    "unit_number": 2,
    "topic_code": "2.10",
    "topic_title": "Inverses of Exponential Functions",
    "stem": "Let f(x) = 5ˣ. Which expression gives f⁻¹(x), obtained by interchanging x and y in y = 5ˣ and solving for y?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "1/5ˣ",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Reciprocal–inverse confusion: This expression is the reciprocal of f(x), not its inverse function. An inverse reverses the input-output relationship rather than taking the reciprocal of each output. Fix: Swap x and y, then solve the resulting exponential equation for y."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "log₅(x)",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Interchanging x and y gives x = 5ʸ. By the definition of a logarithm, y = log₅(x), so f⁻¹(x) = log₅(x) for x > 0."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "x/5",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Exponent–coefficient confusion: Dividing by 5 would undo y = 5x, not y = 5ˣ. Here the unknown appears in the exponent, so solving requires a logarithm. Fix: Identify whether the unknown is multiplied by the base or appears in its exponent."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "log₅(x−1)",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Unnecessary-shift error: Subtracting 1 treats f(0) = 1 as an added constant, but 5ˣ has no vertical shift. After swapping variables, the equation is x = 5ʸ, not x−1 = 5ʸ. Fix: Subtract only constants explicitly added outside the exponential expression."
     }
    ]
   },
